//! Z.ai (GLM), shipped with the harness.
//!
//! The second embedded provider, on the same contract as the first: an
//! ordinary provider script that happens to live in the binary, loaded
//! before `config.toml` and `providers_dir`, replaced whole by a `zai.rn`
//! of your own, and patched a field at a time by a `[[providers]]` table
//! called `zai`. Nothing here is privileged.
//!
//! ## The coding endpoint, not the metered one, not the Anthropic shim
//!
//! z.ai answers on three surfaces: `…/api/paas/v4`, its own chat-completions
//! wire billed per token; `…/api/coding/paas/v4`, the same wire entitled by
//! a GLM Coding Plan subscription; and `…/api/anthropic`, a translation shim
//! documented for pointing Claude Code at GLM. This takes the second.
//!
//! All three were probed with a real key on 2026-09-06. The coding path
//! answered `200` on `glm-5.3`, `glm-4.7` and `glm-4.6`; the metered path
//! answered `429`, code `1113`, *insufficient balance or no resource
//! package*, on all three — because a Coding Plan key funds the plan and
//! not a token balance, and the two are separate purchases. Defaulting to
//! the surface a subscriber's key does not cover would be a built-in that
//! reaches nothing, which is the one thing a built-in is for. Going the
//! other way — an account with API credits and no plan — is a
//! `[[providers]]` patch naming `base_url`, and the prices below are
//! already that surface's.
//!
//! The shim is the more tempting third option on paper, since the Anthropic
//! wire carries `thinking` and cache breakpoints as fields rather than as
//! quirks. What rules it out is the auth header. On `Wire::Anthropic` the
//! harness sends one of two things and neither is what z.ai documents:
//! `auth: "bearer"` adds `anthropic-beta: oauth-2025-04-20` beside the
//! bearer — a claim about Anthropic's own OAuth beta, meaningless to a
//! translator — and `auth: "api_key"` sends `x-api-key` instead of the
//! `Authorization: Bearer` every z.ai surface asks for. On `Wire::OpenAi`
//! the bearer is unconditional and `auth` is never read, which is why
//! there is no `auth` line below.
//!
//! ## Thinking arrives without being asked for, and has to go back
//!
//! `compat.reasoning_effort` is off, deliberately: the harness sends
//! `"medium"` for it and z.ai's enum is `max|high|low`, so the one flag
//! that looks made for this endpoint would put an invalid value on every
//! turn that asked to think. GLM's own spelling is `thinking: #{ type }`,
//! which this wire has no field for — so the harness's `thinking_budget`
//! does not reach the model here.
//!
//! That costs less than it sounds, and the cost was measured rather than
//! guessed: an ordinary turn asking for no thinking at all came back with
//! 41 `reasoning_content` deltas and `reasoning_tokens: 27` in its usage.
//! GLM thinks on its own default, harnox turns those deltas into
//! `ThinkingDelta`, and the reasoning is on screen folded into a cluster
//! like anything else. What is missing is the *switch*, not the thinking. A `request(body)`
//! hook is where that would go if it is ever wanted: turn
//! `compat.reasoning_effort` on to mark the turns that asked for it, then
//! rewrite that field into `thinking` before it reaches the wire.
//!
//! What *does* have to be said is `compat.reasoning_content`. z.ai's
//! thinking-mode page: preserved thinking is on by default on the Coding
//! Plan endpoint — this one — and in that mode "you must return the
//! complete, unmodified reasoning_content back to the API", "together
//! with the tool results", or "performance may degrade and cache hit
//! rates may be affected". The chat-completions body harnox builds leaves
//! thinking out, so without the flag every continuation call in a tool
//! loop handed GLM a transcript with its own reasoning cut out, on the
//! one surface whose default mode says not to. It was noticed on a
//! 2026-09-06 session whose continuation replies came back with no
//! reasoning at all and whose last one wrote its drafts into the visible
//! channel until it looped — not proven to be the cause, but the
//! documented degradation, and the flag is what the documentation asks
//! for.
//!
//! ## The numbers, which this endpoint does not charge
//!
//! Prices are dollars per million tokens from z.ai's pricing page,
//! 2026-09-06 — the *metered* rates. A Coding Plan turn is billed against
//! the plan's quota and not per token, so what the session meter shows on
//! this base URL is not a bill: it is what the same turns would have cost
//! on the API, which is the number worth having when the question is
//! whether the plan earns its subscription. A patch moving `base_url` to
//! `…/api/paas/v4` turns them back into money without touching this file.
//!
//! There is no separate cache-write rate because z.ai caches on its own
//! account and bills only the hit — the same shape as DeepSeek, and the
//! reason `cache_write` is zero rather than absent. It caches without
//! being asked and reports the hit where harnox already looks first —
//! `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`, which read 2048 on the second
//! turn of a session and 2112 on the third — so no `compat` flag is needed
//! to get a prefix reused here, and none is set. The flash row is a
//! standing 50% promotion and is the first thing to check when an estimate
//! looks wrong.
//!
//! Context windows are the ones z.ai's own listing reports through
//! OpenRouter, whose model pages state them and whose own do not; where
//! that listing gives two numbers the smaller is taken.
//!
//! Two more things this endpoint spells its own way, neither set here:
//! `temperature` runs 0.0–1.0 rather than OpenAI's 0–2, so a
//! `[[models]] temperature` above 1 is a `400`, and `max_tokens` has a
//! per-model ceiling — 262144 on `glm-5.3`, 131072 on most, and 32768 on
//! `glm-4.6v`, which is under the harness's own 64000 default and is what
//! `request` below is for.
//!
//! `vision: false` on the text rows is a positive claim and not a shrug —
//! it is what turns an attached screenshot into a line of text on the way
//! out rather than a rejected request for the rest of the branch. The two
//! rows that can see say so. A row added here that nobody has checked
//! should say nothing at all.
//!
//! ## What the plan has left
//!
//! A Coding Plan is two allowances on two clocks — so much every five
//! hours, so much a week — and on this surface those, not the dollar
//! estimate above, are what decide whether the next turn runs. `quota()`
//! reads them from `…/api/monitor/usage/quota/limit`, the call z.ai's own
//! subscription page makes: undocumented, but the same bearer as the chat
//! surface, and a shape that has held across the clients that watch it.
//! Probed with a real Lite key on 2026-09-06:
//!
//! ```json
//! { "code": 200, "success": true, "data": { "level": "lite", "limits": [
//!   { "type": "CREDIT_LIMIT", "unit": 3, "number": 5, "usage": 2000,
//!     "currentValue": 158, "remaining": 1841, "percentage": 7,
//!     "nextResetTime": 1788693968953 },
//!   { "type": "CREDIT_LIMIT", "unit": 6, "number": 1, "usage": 10000,
//!     "currentValue": 158, "remaining": 9841, "percentage": 1,
//!     "nextResetTime": 1789280653997 } ] } }
//! ```
//!
//! A window is a `(unit, number)` pair — `3` hours, `4` days, `6` weeks,
//! `5` months, the numbering z.ai's page uses — so `(3, 5)` is the
//! five-hour window and `(6, 1)` the weekly one, and the label the
//! harness shows is made from the pair rather than assumed, so a plan
//! metered differently reads correctly without an edit here. `usage` is
//! the allowance and `currentValue` what is used, in a quantity the
//! documentation never names (a Lite window is 2000 of it), which is
//! why the reading passes them on as bare counts and leads with the
//! percentage. Older plans spelled the same entry `TOKENS_LIMIT`; both
//! are taken. A `TIME_LIMIT` entry is the monthly web-search count and
//! is left out — nothing here searches the web through z.ai. A key with
//! no plan behind it answers `success: false` with a sentence and no
//! `data`, and that sentence is what the reading reports.
//!
//! The URL is absolute because `base_url` is the chat surface and this
//! path lives beside it, not under it.
//!
//! The key: `eidolon secret set zai < key.txt`, or `ZAI_API_KEY` in the
//! environment. The store wins; the variable is the fallback.

pub fn provider() {
    #{
        name: "zai",
        wire: "openai",
        base_url: "https://api.z.ai/api/coding/paas/v4",
        token_secret: "zai",
        token_env: "ZAI_API_KEY",
        compat: #{ reasoning_content: true },
        models: [
            #{
                id: "glm-5.3",
                name: "GLM 5.3",
                context: 1048576,
                reasoning: true,
                vision: false,
                cost: #{ input: 1.4, output: 4.4, cache_read: 0.26, cache_write: 0.0 },
            },
            #{
                id: "glm-5.3-flash",
                name: "GLM 5.3 Flash",
                context: 1048576,
                reasoning: true,
                vision: true,
                cost: #{ input: 0.075, output: 0.25, cache_read: 0.015, cache_write: 0.0 },
            },
            #{
                id: "glm-4.7",
                name: "GLM 4.7",
                context: 202752,
                reasoning: true,
                vision: false,
                cost: #{ input: 0.6, output: 2.2, cache_read: 0.11, cache_write: 0.0 },
            },
            #{
                id: "glm-4.6v",
                name: "GLM 4.6V",
                context: 131072,
                reasoning: true,
                vision: true,
                cost: #{ input: 0.3, output: 0.9, cache_read: 0.05, cache_write: 0.0 },
            },
            // The rest of the catalogue — glm-5.2, glm-5.1, glm-5,
            // glm-4.7-flash, glm-4.6, glm-4.5-air, glm-4.5v and the free
            // flash rows — is reachable by adding a row here in your own
            // zai.rn. These four are the ones worth a picker slot: the
            // flagship, the cheap one, the last generation's workhorse and
            // a pair of eyes.
        ],
    }
}

/// The largest `max_tokens` a model here will accept, or zero for the ones
/// that take whatever the harness asks for.
///
/// `glm-4.6v` answers `400`, code `1210` — *the max_tokens parameter is
/// illegal, 限制数值范围[1,32768]* — to anything above 32768, and the
/// harness's default is 64000 (`config::default_max_tokens`, raised there
/// because the current models write far past the old 8192 ceiling). So
/// without the clamp below the vision row is not a slow path or an
/// occasional failure: it is a `400` on the first turn of every session
/// that has not lowered `max_tokens` by hand, which is a dead row in the
/// picker wearing the look of a live one.
fn output_cap(model) {
    if model == "glm-4.6v" {
        return 32768;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// Bring `max_tokens` down to what the model in play will take.
///
/// The escape hatch the `compat` flags cannot reach, for the same reason
/// OpenRouter's script needs one: every flag up there is a claim about the
/// whole provider, and this is a claim about one model. It only ever
/// *lowers* the number — a cap is the endpoint's limit, not an opinion
/// about how long an answer should be — so a config that already asks for
/// less than the cap is left alone, and a model with no cap is untouched.
pub fn request(body) {
    let model = match body.get("model") {
        Some(m) => m,
        None => return body,
    };
    let cap = output_cap(model);
    if cap == 0 {
        return body;
    }
    let want = match body.get("max_tokens") {
        Some(n) => n,
        None => return body,
    };
    if want > cap {
        body["max_tokens"] = cap;
    }
    return body;
}

/// Where the plan's meters are read from. See the module docs.
fn quota_url() {
    "https://api.z.ai/api/monitor/usage/quota/limit"
}

/// What the plan has left, read from the account now.
///
/// The harness runs this live and off its own thread (never at load), so
/// there is nothing to cache and no launch waiting on it. The reply is a
/// reading or `#{ error }`; a failed GET is reported with the harness's
/// own sentence about it.
pub fn quota() {
    let body = match eidolon::http_get(quota_url()) {
        Ok(b) => b,
        Err(e) => return #{ error: e },
    };
    return read_quota(body);
}

/// The reading in a quota reply, or a sentence about why there is none.
///
/// Apart from `quota()` so it can be run against a captured body with no
/// network in the way — which is how the harness tests it.
fn read_quota(body) {
    let reply = match json::from_string(body) {
        Ok(v) => v,
        Err(_) => return #{ error: "the quota reply was not JSON" },
    };
    if !(reply is Object) {
        return #{ error: "the quota reply was not an object" };
    }
    let data = match reply.get("data") {
        Some(d) => d,
        None => return #{ error: reply_message(reply) },
    };
    if !(data is Object) {
        return #{ error: reply_message(reply) };
    }
    let limits = match data.get("limits") {
        Some(l) => l,
        None => return #{ error: "the quota reply lists no limits" },
    };
    if !(limits is Vec) {
        return #{ error: "the quota reply lists no limits" };
    }
    let windows = [];
    for entry in limits {
        if !(entry is Object) {
            continue;
        }
        if !is_allowance(entry) {
            continue;
        }
        let unit = int_field(entry, "unit");
        let count = int_field(entry, "number");
        if !(unit is i64) || !(count is i64) {
            continue;
        }
        windows.push(#{
            label: window_label(unit, count),
            percent: percent_of(entry),
            used: int_field(entry, "currentValue"),
            limit: int_field(entry, "usage"),
            resets_ms: int_field(entry, "nextResetTime"),
        });
    }
    let plan = match data.get("level") {
        Some(l) => l,
        None => (),
    };
    if !(plan is String) {
        plan = ();
    }
    return #{ plan: plan, windows: windows };
}

/// Whether a `limits` entry is one of the percentage windows.
fn is_allowance(entry) {
    let kind = match entry.get("type") {
        Some(t) => t,
        None => return false,
    };
    if !(kind is String) {
        return false;
    }
    return kind == "CREDIT_LIMIT" || kind == "TOKENS_LIMIT";
}

/// The name of a `(unit, number)` window: `5h`, `week`, `2w`, `month`.
/// An unknown unit is spelled as the pair rather than dropped, so a new
/// window z.ai adds is still a row with a reset time on it.
fn window_label(unit, count) {
    match unit {
        3 => `${count}h`,
        4 => if count == 1 { "day" } else { `${count}d` },
        6 => if count == 1 { "week" } else { `${count}w` },
        5 => if count == 1 { "month" } else { `${count}mo` },
        _ => `${count}×unit${unit}`,
    }
}

/// An integer field, or `()` — *the endpoint did not say* — when it is
/// absent or not a whole number. The reading's optional fields take
/// `()` as absent.
fn int_field(entry, key) {
    match entry.get(key) {
        Some(v) => if v is i64 { v } else { () },
        None => (),
    }
}

/// The share used, as the endpoint reports it; zero when it does not.
fn percent_of(entry) {
    match entry.get("percentage") {
        Some(p) => if p is i64 || p is f64 { p } else { 0 },
        None => 0,
    }
}

/// The endpoint's own sentence, for a reply with no reading in it: a
/// valid key with no Coding Plan behind it answers `success: false` with
/// a `msg` and no `data`.
fn reply_message(reply) {
    match reply.get("msg") {
        Some(m) => if m is String { m } else { "the quota reply carried no reading" },
        None => "the quota reply carried no reading",
    }
}
